{
  "dom_id": "step-e4647",
  "name": "Example: Identifying the Reflection of a Sine or Cosine Curve Over the Vertical Axis",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933758/assets/370671ebb1a5379bc6688a28d886fe83cf9e4a47e3d1e5444df814bad52fde53.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=2\\sin(kx).$ What is the value of $k?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933758/assets/097b49a161514a80c99e2b66a025289257bf6877ec2cb25a786c5c68f52ea1b3.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\sin x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis.\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=2\\sin(-x).$ Hence, $k=-1.$\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n2\\sin(-x) = -2\\sin x .\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-2\\sin x.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
